@database "stategraph_machine"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: stategraph_machine.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "A machine as an agent"
@{h1}A machine as an agent

A @{" State Graph " link stategraph/main} machine is a workflow written
down as states and transitions. This plugin lets everything that talks to
agents talk to a machine instead: a sub-agent manager (SAM) spawns it, the
chat lists it, another agent calls it by name, @{tt}agent-cli --agent@{utt}
sends it a message. The agent takes one message, starts one run of its
machine through a @{tt}stategraph@{utt} instance -- so the State Graph
panel shows, pauses and terminates that run like any other -- and answers
with the run's output as a JSON object. It runs no LLM of its own.

A machine becomes an agent in one of two ways: an @{tt}agent:@{utt} block
in the machine's own file, or an entry of @{tt}type: stategraph_machine@{utt}
in the config. The plugin has no tools, no hooks and no panel of its own;
the State Graph panel's inspector shows, under "As an agent", which agents
run a machine and the block that would offer it.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Offering a machine as an agent  " link setup}
  @{"  What a request does  " link requests}
  @{"  When the run waits for an event  " link waits}

For agents and developers
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Offering a machine as an agent"
@{h1}Offering a machine as an agent

@{h2}An agent: block in the machine's file

No config entry needed. Every process that starts (the API, agent-cli)
reads the machine folders of each enabled @{tt}stategraph@{utt} instance
and declares an agent for every machine file with a top-level
@{tt}agent:@{utt} block. The machine format itself is described in
@{" Machines " link stategraph/machines}.

@{code yaml}
stategraph: 1
id: build_review
title: Build and review
params:
  task: {type: string, required: true, description: What to build}
agent:                 # {} takes every default
  visibility: tool     # a SAM may offer it to its model
...
@{body}

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
name | <machine id>_agent | the agent's name
description | the machine's title, else its description, else "Runs the state machine <id>" | what callers read about it
input | text when the machine has exactly one param and it is of type string or any, else json | text: the message is one param; json: the message is a JSON object of params
task_param | that one param, else task | the param a text message fills
on_wait | ask | ask or block, see @{" When the run waits " link waits}
params | {} | params every run gets; the message's win over them
promote | [] | output keys copied onto the top level of the final event
visibility | private | private: only callers that name it; tool: a SAM offers it to its model; ui: the chat lists it; both
@{body}

The block is read when a process starts: a new or changed block takes
effect with the next start, and a config reload keeps what the start
declared. A file that does not parse, whose @{tt}id@{utt} is not its file
name, or that is too large offers nothing. Where several stategraph
instances share a machine folder, the first by name offers it.

Validation warns (@{tt}SG111@{utt}, the machine itself still runs) when
the block's @{tt}task_param@{utt} is no param of the machine, when a
required param is neither in @{tt}params@{utt} nor the task param, and when
the name is taken: by another server, or by a config entry for this same
machine -- then the entry's settings run, not the block's.

@{h2}A config entry

The same agent as an entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} or an agent
file. No entry ships with the repository; this one is an example. The keys
sit next to @{tt}type@{utt}, not under @{tt}agent_config@{utt}:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    build_review_agent:
      type: stategraph_machine
      enabled: true
      description: "Builds a change and has it reviewed; answers {change} as JSON"
      machine: build_review      # never taken from the message
      stategraph: stategraph     # the stategraph instance that runs it
      input: text
      task_param: task
      params: {}
      promote: []
      on_wait: block
      metadata:
        visibility: tool
@{body}

@{table}
Key | Default in code | Meaning
machine | none: every request is refused | the machine id
stategraph | stategraph | the stategraph instance whose runs these are
input | text | text or json, as above
task_param | task | the param a text message fills
on_wait | block | ask or block -- note: the block's default is ask
params | {} | params every run gets
promote | [] | output keys copied onto the final event
metadata.visibility | private | as above
@{body}

A wrong value (no @{tt}machine@{utt}, an @{tt}input@{utt} or
@{tt}on_wait@{utt} outside its two words, @{tt}params@{utt} not a mapping,
@{tt}promote@{utt} not a list) does not stop the start: it is logged, and
it is the answer to every request. Shortly after a stategraph instance
starts, it checks every machine agent over it and logs an error for each
one that cannot run: a missing machine, a machine with errors, a
@{tt}task_param@{utt} the machine does not declare, a required param
nothing passes.

@{h2}Who can reach it

With the default @{tt}private@{utt}, a SAM leaves it out of the agents its
model may start, even when @{tt}allowed_agents@{utt} names it, and the chat
does not list it; callers that name it directly still reach it. With
@{tt}tool@{utt} or @{tt}both@{utt}, a SAM offers it like any agent -- the
rest is in @{" Sub-agents and the SAM " link scarabhive/subagents}. A
machine agent can never be run by a machine (see
@{" Security " link stategraph/security}).
@endnode

@node requests "What a request does"
@{h1}What a request does

@{h2}One run per request

A message starts one run of the machine. Its run key is
@{tt}<agent>:<request id>@{utt}, its run id @{tt}<request id>_sg<6
characters>@{utt}: cancel, status lines and cost stay under the request
that sent the message. The run belongs to the request's user; a session
holding another user's run is refused. The run gets its own session,
@{tt}sg_<run id>@{utt} (see @{" Runs " link stategraph/runs}); when the
agent was spawned in a session (a SAM, the chat), that session sits below
the agent's, and the run's agents count one sub-agent level below it, so a
SAM's @{tt}max_nesting_depth@{utt} bounds them.

@{table}
Situation | What happens
the message fits the params | the run starts; the request waits for its end (or, with on_wait ask, for a wait)
the message does not fit | refused; the error lists what the machine takes
the same request again (a retry, a crash) | attaches to its live run, resumes it when it was interrupted, or answers its outcome again -- output, failure or cancel. Only a transient failure (interrupted, timeout, agent_failed, decision_failed, internal, diverged) starts a new run
the run is held by another process | waits up to 90 seconds for it, then refuses
a new message in the same session | never a second run: a succeeded run answers its output again, an interrupted one is resumed, a failed or cancelled one is refused ("a continue does not start a new run"). The session's run is kept in runs.db, so this works in any process
a cancel before the run started | answers cancelled, starts nothing
a cancel of the request, or of a request above it | the run is terminated and its finally activities run; the platform force-cancels the request after its cleanup timeout (10 s by default); the answer is cancelled
the process stops, or the client goes away | the run is left alone and ends interrupted; the same request again resumes it
the machine fails | an error, never a final answer
@{body}

@{h2}Called as another agent's tool

Every call is a request of its own: the request id gets a suffix, the
session is a new one (else a second call would get the first call's
answer), and a run that waits for an event blocks the call whatever
@{tt}on_wait@{utt} says -- a tool call has no conversation to ask in.

@{h2}Input

@{bullet}@{tt}input: text@{utt} -- the whole message is
@{tt}params[task_param]@{utt}, over the fixed @{tt}params@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}input: json@{utt} -- the message is a JSON object of params,
laid over the fixed @{tt}params@{utt}. Anything else is refused.

The params are checked as for any run: a param the machine does not
declare, a required one missing, a value of the wrong type or outside its
enum refuses the start.
@endnode

@node waits "When the run waits for an event"
@{h1}When the run waits for an event

A machine can wait in a state until an event comes: an approval, a
rejection with notes. @{tt}on_wait@{utt} decides what the agent does then.

@{h2}on_wait: block

The request waits until the run ends. The event comes from elsewhere: the
State Graph panel, @{tt}stategraph_send_event@{utt}
(@{" Tools: debugging a run " link stategraph/tools_debug}), a
@{" callback URL " link stategraph/callbacks}. This is the config entry's
default; a caller that takes every answer for the result needs it.

@{h2}on_wait: ask

The turn ends with a question: which state waits (and its description),
the events it takes with their descriptions and data schema, and how to
reply. The final event carries @{tt}waiting: {states, events}@{utt}.

@{code}
build_review waits for an answer in 'approval'.
It takes:
- approve: A person accepts the change
- reject: A person sends it back with notes (data: {"type": "string"})
Reply with the event's name, or with JSON {"event": "<name>", "data": ...} to send data along.
@{body}

The next message in the session is the answer:

@{bullet}the event's name (case does not matter), its data after a space,
a colon or a line break: @{tt}reject: needs a test@{utt} -- data that
parses as JSON is sent as JSON, anything else as text;
@{bullet}or @{tt}{"event": "reject", "data": "needs a test"}@{utt};
@{bullet}where several frames (parallel submachines) take the event, the
question names them and the answer adds @{tt}"frame": "<frame>"@{utt}.

A name the run does not take, or an event it refuses, brings the question
again, headed @{tt}Not sent: <reason>.@{utt}; an event no transition took
(its guards said no) brings it headed @{tt}Not taken: ...@{utt} with the
guards and their results. A taken event runs the machine on to its end or
its next wait. After a restart -- or from agent-cli, which is a process per
message -- the answer resumes the run into its wait and answers it there.
A timer state that takes no event is waited out, not asked about.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The agent

A caller sees an ordinary agent: its name and the @{tt}description@{utt}
of the block or entry, in a SAM's list when it is visible there. The
machine's params are not shown -- write them into the description, or let
the first wrong message's error tell them. A follow-up in the same session
reaches no LLM: it gets the same answer again, except while an
@{tt}on_wait: ask@{utt} run waits.

@{h2}Answers

A succeeded run answers its output as one JSON object, e.g.
@{tt}{"change": "..."}@{utt}; an output that is not an object comes as
@{tt}{"output": ...}@{utt}. The final event also carries
@{tt}run_id@{utt} and the @{tt}promote@{utt} keys. It is not cut: a
machine with a large output hands all of it to its caller.

Errors, verbatim (a SAM shows them as @{tt}Error: ...@{utt}):

@{bullet}"build_review_agent: the machine build_review did not start:
build_review has no parameter(s) topic; it takes: task (string, required)
-- What to build" (with @{tt}input: json@{utt}; a param the agent fills
itself is marked "set by the agent")
@{bullet}"build_review_agent: the message must be a JSON object of params,
not list; it takes: ..."
@{bullet}"build_review_agent: run r1_sgab12cd failed in failed: agent_failed:
... -- {"reason": ...}" (a failed final's own output follows the dash)
@{bullet}"build_review_agent: run r1_sgab12cd was interrupted in build; send
the same request again to resume it"
@{bullet}"build_review_agent: run r1_sgab12cd is cancelled; a continue does
not start a new run"
@{bullet}"build_review_agent: run r1_sgab12cd of this request runs in
<owner>; send the request there or again later"
@{bullet}"build_review_agent: no stategraph instance 'stategraph' is
running"
@{bullet}"build_review_agent: no machine configured (machine: <machine
id>)"
@{bullet}"session s1 is busy with another request; send again when it is
done"
@{bullet}cancelled: "run r1_sgab12cd was cancelled in build"

Every error carries the run id where there is one: the State Graph panel
opens the run.

@{h2}The status line

While the run works: @{tt}build_review r1_sgab12cd: review@{utt} at each
new state. At the end: "build_review r1_sgab12cd: succeeded in done, 42
chars", "...: waits for approve, reject", "...: failed in failed: ...",
"...: cancelled in build", "...: interrupted in build" -- and the lines of
the run's own agents in between.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

The agent calls no model. Its answer reaches the caller as any agent's
answer does, appended; nothing earlier changes. Each exchange is kept in
the agent's session as a user and an assistant message.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}Called as a tool, the agent runs in a fresh session: its run's
session does not sit below the caller's, and the SAM's nesting limit does
not reach the run's agents.
@{bullet}A @{tt}promote@{utt} key named @{tt}type@{utt}, @{tt}summary@{utt}
or @{tt}run_id@{utt} overwrites that field of the final event.
@endnode
